<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Henneberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1078 CE – 1583 CE (approx.) · capital Schleusingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Franconian county in the Thuringian Forest whose princely counts ruled from Schleusingen until their line died out in 1583.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Henneberg family appears in the late 11th century; its counts held the burgraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and built Henneberg castle south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>. Their lands lay between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkalden">Schmalkalden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleusingen">Schleusingen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts divided into several lines, including Henneberg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleusingen">Schleusingen</a>, raised to princely count in 1310. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-vii-count-of-henneberg-schleusingen">Berthold VII</a> was an important adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Louis IV</a>. The counts adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ernest-count-of-henneberg-schleusingen">George Ernest</a> founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at Schleusingen in 1577.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A treaty of 1554 settled inheritance on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a>, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ernest-count-of-henneberg-schleusingen">George Ernest</a> died without heirs in 1583, the county passed to the Ernestine and Albertine branches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, with parts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Its lands later formed parts of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-henneberg.jpg" alt="County of Henneberg" width="320" height="374"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_house_of_Hennenberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1078 CE – 1583 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schleusingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Berthold VII of Henneberg, George Ernest of Henneberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Princely County of Henneberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=county-of-henneberg" aria-label="County of Henneberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4925 423 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Henneberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5245 679L5229 694M5245 679L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5245 679h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5245 679h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schleusingen. <a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=county-of-henneberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
